The MITRE ATT&CK technique "Pass the Hash" (PtH) is a method adversaries use to authenticate as a user without needing the user's cleartext password. Instead of using the actual password, attackers capture and reuse the valid password hash of an account to bypass standard authentication processes that require the cleartext password.

This technique allows attackers to move laterally within a network environment by leveraging stolen password hashes, effectively bypassing normal system access controls. By passing the hash, adversaries can impersonate users and gain unauthorized access to systems and resources without needing to crack or know the actual password.

Detection and mitigation strategies typically focus on protecting credential hashes, limiting administrative privileges, enforcing multi-factor authentication, and monitoring for unusual authentication patterns indicative of pass-the-hash activity.

